&lt;p>They could also be very bad and use some crawler to try to bring down our server.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>So how to do that?&lt;/p>
&lt;p>In this article, we use springboot and log the user&amp;rsquo;s information and access frequency into redis, you can also store it into memory or database.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>Oauth2 Single Login</title><link>https://travisbikkle.github.io/en/2024/07/oauth2-single-login/</link><pubDate>Fri, 19 Jul 2024 10:20:30 +0800</pubDate><author>zhaoyu025@gmail.com (Travis Bikkle)</author><guid>https://travisbikkle.github.io/en/2024/07/oauth2-single-login/</guid><category domain="https://travisbikkle.github.io/en/categories/back-end/">Back-End</category><description>&lt;p>Sometimes we want users to be able to log in to their accounts on only one device (we&amp;rsquo;re too stingy).&lt;/p>
